# =============================================================================
# This repository is PUBLIC (or will be — see the repo README). Everything below
# is excluded because it either is a secret, contains one, or is an organization's
# private asset that a public fork must never carry.
#
# Flipping a repository to public exposes its ENTIRE HISTORY, not just the tip.
# A secret committed today and deleted tomorrow is still public forever. That is
# why these rules exist from the first commit rather than being added later.
#
# `gitleaks` runs in CI over every push and pull request as the mechanical
# backstop for this file (.github/workflows/secrets.yml).
# =============================================================================

# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The real .env is rendered on the VM at deploy time from Azure Key Vault by
# scripts/render-env.sh and never exists in this repository.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Keys and certificates of every shape.
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.jwk
*.jwks
!*.pub

# --- Organization assets ---------------------------------------------------
# Letterhead templates are an organization's private branding and are read at
# runtime from the data disk (LETTERHEAD_DIR), never from git. See
# docs/modules/mcp-letterwriter.md.
*.docx
*.doc

# --- Data ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mongo dumps taken during a migration or a restore test are a full copy of
# confidential client data.
*.archive
*.archive.gz
*.dump
dump/
mongodump*/
librechat.runtime.yaml

# --- Local tooling ---------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
.venv/
site/
__pycache__/

# --- OS / editor cruft -----------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~

# Filled-in deployment parameters name your machine, your admin source
# addresses, and your alert recipients. Keep them out of a public repository.
infra/main.parameters.json

# --- Internal planning documents -------------------------------------------
# These are working notes for the people running a specific deployment, and they
# routinely carry exactly what a public repository must not: subscription IDs,
# tenant IDs, resource IDs, and the addresses of production hosts.
#
# Keep them next to the repository, not in it. If some of the content belongs in
# public documentation, rewrite that part into docs/ with the identifiers taken
# out, rather than committing the original.
IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md
MIGRATION_PLAN.md
HANDOFF.md
START-HERE.md
*-recon.md
*-internal.md

# --- Exported data -----------------------------------------------------------
# Anything exported from an identity provider, a directory, or the application
# itself. These carry personal data — names, work email addresses, IP addresses,
# device identifiers — and none of it belongs in a public repository.
#
# Do not rely on `gitleaks` or push protection to catch these: personal data is
# not a credential, so nothing in the automated chain is looking for it. This
# block is the only control that applies.
#
# The failure mode to guard against is `git add -A` in a directory where an
# export happens to be sitting. Keep exports somewhere else entirely; this is
# the backstop, not the plan.
*.csv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*signins*
*-export-*
*-audit-*
